    int n = mat.length;
    int[] res = new int[n * n];
    int i = 0;
    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
        for (int k = 0; k <= j; k++) {
            res[i++] = mat[j][k];
        }
    }
    for (int j = n - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
        for (int k = n - 1; k > j; k--) {
            res[i++] = mat[j][k];
        }
    }
    return res;
}
```
This function works by first iterating through the rows of the matrix, and for each row, iterating through the elements of that row in order (from left to right) and storing them in the result array. It then repeats this process for the other half of the matrix, starting with the last row and iterating through the elements in that row in reverse order (from right to left).

For example, if we apply this function to the matrix
```
[1, 2, 3]
[4, 5, 6]
[7, 8, 9]
```
it will return the array
```
[1, 2, 4, 7, 5, 3, 6, 8, 9]
